## Local Setup

```bash
# 1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/your-username/korea-ish-elonlari-bot.git
cd korea-ish-elonlari-bot

# 2. Install dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 3. Configure environment
cp .env.example .env
# Edit .env and fill in all required values

# 4. Generate a session string (first time only)
python generate_session.py --acct 1 --qr

# 5. Run the bot
python main.py
```

---

## Generating a Session String

A session string lets the bot log in to your Telegram account without storing
a local session file — essential for cloud deployment on Railway.

```bash
# QR code login (recommended — no SMS needed)
python generate_session.py --acct 1 --qr
python generate_session.py --acct 2 --qr

# Phone number login (fallback)
python generate_session.py --acct 1 --phone

# Interactive menu
python generate_session.py
```

The script prints `SESSION_STRING=<value>` when complete. Copy the value into
your `.env` file or Railway Variables.

> **QR login:** Open Telegram on your phone → Settings → Devices →
> Link Desktop Device → scan the QR code shown in the terminal.

---

## Railway Deployment

### 1. Push your code

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Initial deploy"
git push
```

Railway detects the push and builds automatically using Nixpacks.

### 2. Set environment variables

In Railway dashboard → your service → **Variables** tab, add all required
variables from the [Environment Variables](#environment-variables) section.

### 3. Attach a persistent volume (critical)

Railway containers have an ephemeral filesystem — without a volume, your
database is deleted on every redeploy.

1. Railway dashboard → your service → **Volumes** tab
2. Click **New Volume**
3. Set Mount Path: `/data`
4. Add variable: `DATABASE_PATH = /data/job_bot.db`

Your database now survives all future deployments permanently.

### 4. Fix legacy group IDs (first deploy only)

If you previously added groups by numeric ID before the ID-fix patch, run the
migration script once via the Railway shell:

```bash
python fix_chat_ids.py
```

