## Admin Bot Commands

Open a chat with your bot and use the keyboard or these commands:

| Button | Action |
|---|---|
| 📋 List Groups | Show all monitored source groups |
| ➕ Add Group | Add a group by @username or numeric ID |
| ➖ Remove Group | Remove a monitored group |
| 🎯 Set Target | Set the forwarding destination group |
| 📊 Status | Show connection status for all accounts |
| 🧪 Test Send | Send a test message to the target group |
| 📈 Stats | Today's forwarding statistics |
| 🔍 Test Keyword | Test whether a text would be detected as a job post |
| 🔎 Check Groups | Verify account membership in all monitored groups |
| 🚫 Blocked Users | View, add, or remove user blocks |

When adding a group, if Account 2 is configured you will be asked which
account should monitor it.

---

## Message Forwarding Pipeline

Every incoming message passes through 9 gates in sequence. It is dropped at
the first gate it fails.

```
Gate 1    Source-group check     Is this chat_id in the monitored list?
                                 (O(1) in-memory frozenset — no DB I/O)
Gate 2    Text extraction        Does the message have text or a caption?
Gate 2.5  Blocked-user check     Is the sender in the blocked list?
                                 (O(1) in-memory set — no DB I/O)
Gate 3    Forward dedup          Is this a Telegram-native forward of an
                                 already-processed message?
Gate 4    Message ID dedup       Have we seen this (chat_id, message_id)?
Gate 5    Keyword filter         Does the text contain a job-signal keyword?
Gate 5.5  Content dedup          Is this text a near-duplicate of a recently
                                 forwarded post?
Gate 6    Entity resolution      Resolve group title/link and sender name
Gate 7    Timestamp              Normalise to UTC, fall back to utcnow()
Gate 8    Target lookup          Get target group ID from in-memory cache
Gate 9    Build and send         Format the post and send
```

The content hash is registered only after a successful send. A failed send
never permanently blacklists content.

---

## Keyword Filter

`bot/filters.py` uses a curated list of strong, unambiguous job-signal phrases
in three languages. A single match is enough to classify a message as a job
post.

**Languages:** Uzbek, Russian, Korean

**Example phrases:**

| Language | Examples |
|---|---|
| Uzbek | `ish bor`, `ishchi kerak`, `vakansiya`, `maosh`, `arbayt`, `ish haqi` |
| Russian | `вакансия`, `нужен работник`, `подработка`, `зарплата от` |
| Korean | `구인`, `채용`, `모집`, `시급`, `알바`, `사람 구해요` |

Text is normalised before matching: lowercased, separators (`-_./`) replaced
with spaces, emoji and punctuation stripped. Korean uses substring matching;
Uzbek and Russian use word-boundary matching.

To add a new keyword, add the phrase to `STRONG_KEYWORDS` in `filters.py` and
restart the bot.

---

## Duplicate Detection

Three independent layers prevent the same job listing from appearing more than
once in the target group.

**Layer 1 — Telegram forward header (Gate 3)**
If a user presses Telegram's Forward button, the message carries a `fwd_from`
header. If that original was already forwarded, the copy is dropped.

**Layer 2 — Message ID (Gate 4)**
Each successfully forwarded `(chat_id, message_id)` is stored in
`processed_msgs`. The same pair seen again is dropped.

**Layer 3 — Content fingerprinting (Gate 5.5)**
Catches the most common real-world pattern: the same listing copied and pasted
manually into multiple groups.

- *Tier 1 — Exact hash:* SHA-256 of normalised text (phone numbers and URLs
  stripped). O(1) indexed DB lookup. Zero false positives.
- *Tier 2 — Fuzzy match:* `difflib.SequenceMatcher` ratio against all texts
  forwarded within the dedup window. Texts scoring above
  `DEDUP_SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD` (default 0.85) are treated as duplicates.

Old fingerprint rows are pruned on startup and nightly by the scheduler.

---

## Database Schema

```sql
source_groups       -- monitored source chats
  id, chat_id UNIQUE, title, username, added_at, assigned_account

target_group        -- forwarding destination (singleton, id = 1)
  id, chat_id, title, username, updated_at

processed_msgs      -- per-message dedup index
  PK(chat_id, message_id), processed_at

forwarded_stats     -- historical forwarding records
  id, source_chat_id, source_title, matched_lang,
  matched_kw, match_tier, forwarded_at

original_msg_index  -- Telegram-native forward origin registry
  PK(fwd_chat_id, fwd_msg_id), seen_at

content_hashes      -- content fingerprints for cross-group dedup
  id, text_hash, norm_text, source_chat, source_msg, seen_at

blocked_users       -- senders whose messages are never forwarded
  user_id PK, full_name, username, reason, blocked_at
```

---

## Utility Scripts

### `generate_session.py`

Generates a Telethon string session for cloud deployment. Supports both QR
code login and phone number login with automatic 2FA handling.

### `fix_chat_ids.py`

One-time migration that corrects source groups stored with a positive
`chat_id` (missing the mandatory `-100` supergroup prefix). Run once after the
first deployment if groups were added with a version of the bot that had this
bug.

---

## Tuning & Configuration

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| Too many unrelated posts forwarded | Remove loose keywords from `STRONG_KEYWORDS` |
| Bot missing job posts in a specific pattern | Add the missing phrase to `STRONG_KEYWORDS` |
| Duplicate posts still appear in target group | Lower `DEDUP_SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD` (e.g. `0.78`) |
| Unique posts incorrectly blocked as duplicates | Raise `DEDUP_SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD` (e.g. `0.92`) |
| Dedup window too short or too long | Adjust `DEDUP_WINDOW_HOURS` |
| Too much log noise in Railway | Set `LOG_LEVEL=WARNING` |
| Forwarding delays visible in logs | Check for `FloodWait` lines — bot handles these automatically |

---

## Known Limitations

- **No media forwarding.** Only text and captions are forwarded. Photos and
  videos are not included by design, to keep the target group clean.
- **Telethon userbot required.** The bot uses personal Telegram accounts, not
  the Bot API. Both accounts must be members of all assigned source groups.
- **Single-process SQLite.** Sufficient for this use case, but not suitable
  for distributed or multi-process deployments. Migrate to PostgreSQL if
  scaling horizontally.
- **Session invalidation.** If Telegram logs out the account (too many
  devices, suspicious activity flag), a new session string must be generated
  and the `SESSION_STRING` variable updated in Railway.
- **Rate limits.** Telegram limits how many messages a userbot can send per
  minute. The bot handles `FloodWaitError` by sleeping the exact required
  duration, but very high-volume bursts may still cause brief delays.

